tel 1.0

base polaris
import soundness

info   fixed-width binary layouts as case classes, read from bytes by a derived typeclass
detail
    _Polaris_ reads structured binary data — the fixed-width records of file headers, network
    packets and device protocols — by mapping bytes onto Scala types. A case class whose fields
    are sized numeric types (`B32`, `U16`, `S64`, …) *is* a binary layout: each field's type
    states its width and its interpretation, and derivation composes them in declaration order,
    computing every offset, so there is no index arithmetic to keep in step with a
    specification.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Header(magic: B32, version: U16, flags: B16, length: U32)

    object Header:
      given Header is Debufferable = Debufferable.derived

    val data = hex"cafebabe0001000000000010"

    byteWidth[Header]                          // 12
    data.unpackFrom[Header](0).length          // 16

    data.sextant:
      val magic = unpack[B32]
      unpack[U16]                              // 1
    ```

    `Debufferable` is the reading typeclass, with instances for the sixteen sized and plain
    numeric types and derivation for products; `Unpackable` sits above it and is what `unpack`
    and `unpackFrom` resolve, covering a single value and a count-prefixed array of them. A
    `Sextant` is the read position, a mutable capability introduced by the `sextant { … }`
    block, so that successive `unpack`s advance through the bytes. `Bufferable` is the writing
    typeclass, deriving over products in the same way.

    All multi-byte numbers are read big-endian. Reads are not bounds-checked against a layout:
    reading past the end of the bytes throws the platform's `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`, so
    check the length of untrusted input against `byteWidth` first.

    The library has one source set, `polaris.core`, which compiles for every platform.
    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer polaris.Debufferable
refer polaris.Unpackable
refer polaris.Sextant
refer polaris.unpackFrom

entry .Sextant   a read position in a byte array: the mutable capability successive `unpack`s advance
  detail
      A `Sextant` is a cursor over `Data`, named for the navigator's instrument that reads a
      position. It is a *capability*: it holds a mutable offset, so unpacking through it is an
      effect, and it is `caps.Mutable`, so only an exclusive reference (`Sextant^`) may advance
      it. Two readers sharing one position would each consume bytes the other expected, and the
      capture checker prevents a sextant from being aliased or from escaping the block which
      introduced it.

      The usual way to get one is the `sextant { … }` extension on `Data`, which makes a sextant
      at offset 0 and passes it as a contextual value to the block, where `unpack` finds it:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val data = hex"cafebabe00010000"

      data.sextant:
        summon[Sextant].advance(4)      // skip the magic number
        val version = unpack[U16]
        summon[Sextant].offset          // 6
      ```

      `Sextant(bytes, initialPosition)` constructs one directly, starting at `initialPosition`
      (default 0). It is `Findable`, so compile-time searches for capabilities in scope can see
      it.
  refer polaris.sextant
  refer polaris.unpack
  entry #offset   the current read position, as a byte index from the start of the data
      Each `unpack` advances it by the width of the value read, so it says where the next read
      will begin, for a format which records where a section starts.
  entry #advance   moves the read position forward by a number of bytes, without reading them
      An `update` method, so it needs an exclusive reference to the sextant: it skips padding,
      or a field this reader does not need. The count is not checked against the length of the
      data; a later read beyond the end throws.

entry .sextant   runs a block with a `Sextant` over these bytes, starting at offset 0
    An extension on `Data`: `data.sextant { … }` makes a `Sextant` at the start of the bytes and
    passes it to the block as a contextual `Sextant^`, so each `unpack` inside reads the next
    value and advances past it. The block's result is returned; the sextant does not escape it.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    hex"00010002".sextant:
      (unpack[Short], unpack[Short])   // (1, 2)
    ```

entry .unpack   reads the next value of a type from the `Sextant` in scope, and advances past it
    `unpack[T]` resolves `T is Unpackable` and reads through the contextual `Sextant^`. For a
    `Debufferable` type the result is the value, and the sextant moves on by its width; for a
    frozen array `Array[T]^{}` the result is a function from a count to the array, which is read
    when the count is known and does not move the caller's sextant.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Pair(left: U16, right: U16)

    object Pair:
      given Pair is Debufferable = Debufferable.derived

    hex"00010002000300040005000600070008".sextant:
      val pairs = unpack[Array[Pair]^{}](4)
      pairs.length   // 4
    ```

    Reading past the end of the data throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.

entry .unpackFrom   reads a value of a chosen type from bytes at an offset
    An extension on `Data`: `bytes.unpackFrom[T](offset)` makes a `Sextant` at `offset` and
    unpacks one `T` through it, for a single read where a `sextant { … }` block would be
    ceremony. As with `unpack`, an array type gives a function from a count to the array.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    hex"090900000100".unpackFrom[Int](2)                         // 256
    hex"000000010000000200000003".unpackFrom[Array[Int]^{}](4)(2)    // the array 2, 3
    ```

    Numbers are read big-endian; reading past the end of the bytes throws
    `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.

entry .byteWidth   the number of bytes a `Debufferable` type occupies, without a value in hand
    `byteWidth[T]` is `T`'s `Debufferable#width`: 1, 2, 4 or 8 for the numeric types, and the sum
    of its fields' widths for a derived product, nested records included.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Header(magic: B32, version: U16, flags: B16, length: U32)

    object Header:
      given Header is Debufferable = Debufferable.derived

    byteWidth[Header]   // 12
    byteWidth[U16]      // 2
    ```

entry .Debufferable   a typeclass for a fixed-width type which can be read from bytes
  detail
      `T is Debufferable` states two things: `#width`, the number of bytes a `T` occupies, and
      `#debuffer`, which reads one from a `Sextant` and advances it by that width. The companion
      has instances for the sized bit types (`B8`–`B64`), the signed and unsigned integers
      (`S8`–`S64`, `U8`–`U64`) and Scala's `Byte`, `Short`, `Int` and `Long`, all big-endian.

      A case class derives its instance from its fields', which are read in declaration order;
      a field whose type is itself a derived record contributes its whole width, so layouts
      compose:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Header(magic: Int, version: Short, flags: Byte)

      object Header:
        given debufferable: Header is Debufferable = Debufferable.derived

      Array[Byte](0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 3, 7).unpackFrom[Header](0)   // Header(256, 3, 7)
      ```

      A layout which is not a product of fixed-width fields — a packed colour, say, or a type
      with its own interpretation of some bytes — defines an instance with `.apply`. Only
      products derive: there is no derivation for sum types, since a binary layout has no
      standard tag.
  refer polaris.Unpackable
  refer polaris.byteWidth
  entry .apply   makes an instance from a byte width and a function of the bytes and an offset
      `Debufferable(width)((bytes, offset) => …)` reads a value starting at `offset` in `bytes`,
      and the instance advances the sextant by `width` afterwards. This is how the primitive
      instances are written.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Rgb(red: Byte, green: Byte, blue: Byte)

      object Rgb:
        given Rgb is Debufferable =
          Debufferable(3): (bytes, offset) =>
            Rgb(bytes.readUnchecked(offset), bytes.readUnchecked(offset + 1), bytes.readUnchecked(offset + 2))
      ```
  entry .b8   reads one byte as a `B8`
  entry .b16   reads two bytes, big-endian, as a `B16`
  entry .b32   reads four bytes, big-endian, as a `B32`
  entry .b64   reads eight bytes, big-endian, as a `B64`
  entry .s8   reads one byte as a signed `S8`
  entry .s16   reads two bytes, big-endian, as a signed `S16`
  entry .s32   reads four bytes, big-endian, as a signed `S32`
  entry .s64   reads eight bytes, big-endian, as a signed `S64`
  entry .u8   reads one byte as an unsigned `U8`
  entry .u16   reads two bytes, big-endian, as an unsigned `U16`
  entry .u32   reads four bytes, big-endian, as an unsigned `U32`
  entry .u64   reads eight bytes, big-endian, as an unsigned `U64`
  entry .byte   reads one byte as a `Byte`
  entry .short   reads two bytes, big-endian and signed, as a `Short`
  entry .int   reads four bytes, big-endian and signed, as an `Int`
  entry .long   reads eight bytes, big-endian and signed, as a `Long`
  entry .Join   the instance derivation builds for a product: a total width and a reader
    detail
        Built by `.conjunction`, which `Debufferable.derived` calls for a case class: its width
        is the sum of the fields' widths, and its reader reads each field in turn through the
        same sextant and constructs the product. Not usually named by user code.
    entry #width   the product's total width in bytes
  entry .conjunction   derives an instance for a product from its fields' instances
      The product half of the `ProductDerivable` derivation, which `Debufferable.derived` invokes;
      user code writes `Debufferable.derived` rather than calling this.
  entry #width   the number of bytes a value of this type occupies
  entry #debuffer   reads one value from a `Sextant` and advances it by `#width`
      Takes an exclusive `Sextant^`, since reading moves its position. A hand-written instance
      must declare its parameter as `Sextant^`.

entry .Unpackable   a typeclass for what `unpack` can read: a single value, or an array by count
  detail
      `unpack` and `unpackFrom` resolve `T is Unpackable`, whose `#Wrap` type says the shape of
      the result: for any `Debufferable` type the result is the value itself (`.debufferable`),
      and for a frozen array `Array[T]^{}` it is `Int -> Array[T]^{}`, a function from a count
      to the array (`.frozenArray`). The second is how a count-prefixed section reads as one
      call, with the count supplied once it is known:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val data = Array[Byte](0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 2, 0, 0, 0, 3)

      data.sextant:
        val read = unpack[Array[Int]^{}]
        (read(1), read(2))   // the arrays 1 and 1, 2
      ```
  refer polaris.Debufferable
  refer polaris.unpack
  entry .frozenArray   reads a frozen array of `Debufferable` elements, as a function from a count
      Provides `Array[T]^{} is Unpackable` for any `T` with a `Debufferable` and a `ClassTag`.
      Its result records the data and the start offset and makes a fresh sextant each time it is
      applied, so the function is pure, may be applied more than once, and never advances the
      caller's sextant: a reader which continues after the array must `advance` past it.
  entry .debufferable   reads a single value of any `Debufferable` type
      Provides `T is Unpackable` for every `T is Debufferable`, with the value itself as the
      result, advancing the sextant by the type's width.
  entry #Wrap   the shape of the result: the value itself, or a function from a count
  entry #Result   the result type `unpack` gives: `Wrap[Self]`
  entry #unpack   reads from a `Sextant`, giving a `Wrap[Self]`

entry .Bufferable   a typeclass for a fixed-width type which can be written to bytes
  detail
      The writing counterpart to `Debufferable`: `T is Bufferable` states the `#width` a `T`
      occupies and `#sextant`, which writes one through a `Sextant`. Products derive their
      instances from their fields' with `Bufferable.derived`, as `Debufferable` does, so a
      layout is declared once for both directions.

      The companion currently declares no instances for the numeric types, so a derived product
      has no field instances to compose until they are supplied.
  refer polaris.Debufferable
  entry .Join   the instance derivation builds for a product: a total width and a writer
    detail
        Built by `.conjunction`, which `Bufferable.derived` calls for a case class: its width is
        the sum of the fields' widths, and its writer writes each field in turn through the same
        sextant. Not usually named by user code.
    entry #width   the product's total width in bytes
  entry .conjunction   derives an instance for a product from its fields' instances
      The product half of the `ProductDerivable` derivation, which `Bufferable.derived` invokes.
  entry #width   the number of bytes a value of this type occupies
  entry #sextant   writes a value through a `Sextant`
      Takes an exclusive `Sextant^`; a hand-written instance must declare its parameter so.
